998</year>. <italic>Drosophila</italic> oocyte localization is mediated by differential cadherin-based adhesion. <source>Nature.</source> <volume>395</volume>:<fpage>387</fpage>&#x02013;391.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9759729</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib12"><citation citation-type="journal">Gonzalez-Reyes, A., and D. St Johnston. <year>1998</year>. The <italic>Drosophila</italic> AP axis is polarised by the cadherin-mediated positioning of the oocyte. <source>Development.</source> <volume>125</volume>:<fpage>3635</fpage>&#x02013;3644.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9716529</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib13"><citation citation-type="journal">Gottardi, C.J., L.A. Dunbar, and M.J. Caplan. <year>1995</year>. Biotinylation and assessment of membrane polarity: caveats and methodological concerns. <source>Am. J. Physiol</source>. <volume>268</volume>:<fpage>F285</fpage>&#x02013;F295.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7864168</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib14"><citation citation-type="journal">Gottardi, C.J., E. Wong, and B.M. Gumbiner. <year>2001</year>. E-cadherin suppresses cellular transformation by inhibiting beta-catenin signaling in an adhesion-independent manner. <source>J. Cell Biol.</source> <volume>153</volume>:<fpage>1049</fpage>&#x02013;1060.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11381089</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib15"><citation citation-type="journal">Gumbiner, B.M. <year>1996</year>. Cell adhesion: the molecular basis of tissue architecture and morphogenesis. <source>Cell.</source> <volume>84</volume>:<fpage>345</fpage>&#x02013;357.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8608588</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib16"><citation citation-type="journal">Huber, A.H., D.B. Stewart, D.V. Laurents, W.J. Nelson, and W.I. Weis. <year>2001</year>. The cadherin cytoplasmic domain is unstructured in the absence of beta-catenin. A possible mechanism for regulating cadherin turnover. <source>J. Biol. Chem.</source> <volume>276</volume>:<fpage>12301</fpage>&#x02013;12309.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11121423</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib17"><citation citation-type="journal">Inuzuka, H., S. Miyatani, and M. Takeichi. <year>1991</year>. R-cadherin: a novel Ca(2+)-dependent cell-cell adhesion molecule expressed in the retina. <source>Neuron.</source> <volume>7</volume>:<fpage>69</fpage>&#x02013;79.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1712604</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib18"><citation citation-type="journal">Islam, S., T.E. Carey, G.T. Wolf, M.J. Wheelock, and K.R. Johnson. <year>1996</year>. Expression of N-cadherin by human squamous carcinoma cells induces a scattered fibroblastic phenotype with disrupted cell-cell adhesion. <source>J. Cell Biol.</source> <volume>135</volume>:<fpage>1643</fpage>&#x02013;1654.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8978829</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib19"><citation citation-type="journal">Kim, J.B., S. Islam, Y.J. Kim, R.S. Prudoff, K.M. Sass, M.J. Wheelock, and K.R. Johnson. <year>2000</year>. N-cadherin extracellular repeat 4 mediates epithelial to mesenchymal transition and increased motility. <source>J. Cell Biol.</source> <volume>151</volume>:<fpage>1193</fpage>&#x02013;1206.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11121435</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib20"><citation citation-type="journal">Laemmli, U.K. <year>1970</year>. Cleavage of structural proteins during the assembly of the head of bacteriophage T4. <source>Nature.</source> <volume>227</volume>:<fpage>680</fpage>&#x02013;685.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">5432063</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib21"><citation citation-type="journal">Larue, L., C. Antos, S. Butz, O. Huber, V. Delmas, M. Dominis, and R. Kemler. <year>1996</year>. A role for cadherins in tissue formation. <source>Development.</source> <volume>122</volume>:<fpage>3185</fpage>&#x02013;3194.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8898231</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib22"><citation citation-type="journal">Levi, G., B. Gumbiner, and J.P. Thiery. <year>1991</year>. The distribution of E-cadherin during <